<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emmanuel Joseph Sieyès</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1748">1748</a>–<a href="/years/1836">1836</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Abbé Sieyès, Sieyès</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emmanuel Joseph Sieyès, usually known as the Abbé Sieyès, was a French Catholic priest and political writer, one of the leading theorists of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frejus">Fréjus</a>, he was educated for the priesthood but drawn to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> thinkers, and rose to be vicar general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a>. His pamphlet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/what-is-the-third-estate">What Is the Third Estate?</a> of January 1789 became the Revolution's political manifesto and helped turn the Estates-General into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> in June 1789. He voted for the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a>, lived through the Terror, became a director in 1799 and was among the instigators of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coup-of-18-brumaire">Coup of 18 Brumaire</a>, which brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> to power. He also coined the term sociologie.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emmanuel-joseph-sieyes.jpg" alt="Emmanuel Joseph Sieyès" width="330" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacques-Louis David · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emmanuel_Joseph_Sieyès,_by_Jacques_Louis_David.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Emmanuel Joseph Sieyès was born on 3 May 1748, the fifth child of a local tax collector of modest income in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frejus">Fréjus</a>. The family claimed some noble blood but were commoners. Frail health thwarted his ambition to be a soldier, and he turned to a religious career, spending ten years at the seminary of Saint-Sulpice in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. He read Locke, Hume, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denis-diderot">Diderot</a> in preference to theology. After serving as secretary to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treguier">Tréguier</a>, he followed him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a> in 1780 as vicar general, and became a canon and chancellor of the diocese. He resented how easily nobles advanced in the Church compared with commoners.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1788 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> proposed to convene the Estates-General, and Sieyès published <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/what-is-the-third-estate">What Is the Third Estate?</a> in January 1789. It began: &quot;What is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-estate">Third Estate</a>? Everything. What has it been hitherto in the political order? Nothing. What does it desire to be? Something.&quot; It argued that the Third Estate represented the nation by itself. Though a priest, he was elected a deputy of the Third Estate for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. He proposed that the other orders join it, and on 5 June 1789 it took the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a>.</p><p>In the Assembly he helped draft the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-the-rights-of-man-and-of-the-citizen">Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen</a> and argued for a single chamber and against a royal veto. In the Convention he voted for the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a>. Asked what he had done during the Terror, he replied, &quot;J'ai vécu&quot; (I lived). He refused a place in the Directory in 1795 but became a director in May 1799.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Sieyès planned to overthrow the Directory and, in the coup of 18 Brumaire, he and his allies dissolved it, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>, Sieyès and Roger Ducos Consuls. The constitution he had long planned was remodelled by Bonaparte into the Constitution of the Year VIII. Sieyès soon retired from the consulship, became one of the first members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senat-conservateur">Sénat conservateur</a> and received an estate at Crosne from Napoleon. Expelled from the Academy of Moral and Political Sciences in 1816 for his role in the king's execution, he lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> until 1830 and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1836. In an unpublished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of 1780 he had coined the term sociologie.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emmanuel Joseph Sieyès” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>(1789) the <b>Abbé Sieyès</b> attacked the French nobility as a privileged caste living off the nation, and in Rights of Man (1791) Thomas Paine called the idea of hereditary legislators “as absurd as an hereditary mathematician”.</blockquote><a href="/government/aristocracy">Aristocracy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>In August 1789 Abbé <b>Emmanuel Joseph Sieyès</b> and Honoré Mirabeau played a central role in conceptualising and drafting the final text.</blockquote><a href="/work/declaration-of-the-rights-of-man-and-of-the-citizen">Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>In January 1789 the <b>Abbé Sieyès</b> asked in a famous pamphlet what the Third Estate was, and answered: “Everything.”</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancien-regime-france">Social Hierarchy of Ancien Régime France</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frejus">Fréjus</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.43,6.74&amp;year=1805" aria-label="Fréjus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Emmanuel Joseph Sieyès"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5417 3849h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5417 3849h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.43,6.74&amp;year=1805">What was here in 1805? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
